<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Schumann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Schumann was a German composer, pianist and music critic of the early Romantic era, born in Zwickau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, who composed in all the main musical genres of his time. He studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and piano with Friedrich Wieck from 1829, but a worsening problem with his right hand turned him to composition, and in 1834 he co-founded the Neue Zeitschrift für Musik, which he edited for ten years. In 1840 he married Wieck's daughter Clara against her father's bitter opposition, then wrote song cycles such as Dichterliebe and, in 1841, the first of his four symphonies. In 1854 he threw himself into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and died two years later in a sanatorium near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-schumann.jpg" alt="Robert Schumann" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Kriehuber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Schumann_1839.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Austria, when <b>Robert Schumann</b> quoted it in 1839, singing it was forbidden as a piece that might cause public disorder.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseph von Eichendorff also used the figure in his poem &quot;Waldesgespräch&quot; (1812), later set by <b>Robert Schumann</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexandrov described the music as a march joined to Russian traditional music, above all the bylina epic songs, and it shares chord progressions with Vasily Kalinnikov's overture Bylina and has been compared with <b>Robert Schumann</b>'s &quot;Frühlingsfahrt&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zwickau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Endenich, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.72,12.5&amp;year=1839" aria-label="Zwickau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4835 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Schumann"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5285 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5285 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.72,12.5&amp;year=1839">What was here in 1839? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Play strictly in time! The playing of many a virtuoso resembles the walk of an intoxicated person.&#8221;</p><footer>Advice to Young Musicians (1850) &#183; tr. Henry Hugo Pierson, 1860 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28219/pg28219-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Endeavour to play easy pieces well and with elegance; that is better than to play difficult pieces badly.&#8221;</p><footer>Advice to Young Musicians (1850) &#183; tr. Henry Hugo Pierson, 1860 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28219/pg28219-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The object of art is not to produce riches. Become a great artist, and all other desirable accessories will fall to your lot.&#8221;</p><footer>Advice to Young Musicians (1850) &#183; tr. Henry Hugo Pierson, 1860 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28219/pg28219-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
